+
+A **task failure** means the robot acted and did not finish the task. An **invalid
+experiment** means the robot never got to act (here: 0 control steps, 0 inference calls),
+so it says nothing about the policy and is not counted as 0/10.
+
+**What the learned result shows (verified from the saved movement traces):**
+- **The policy often lifts the wrong utensil.** It lifted the requested utensil first
+  in only 3 of 10 scenes (seeds 4, 5, 7), in each of the three valid runs. With the
+  supervisor on, seeds 0, 1, 3 and 9 lifted the spare utensil about 8 cm while the
+  requested one never rose above 3.5 cm. These are the same 10 scenes three times, not
+  30 independent scenes. In an open-loop test on the same 10 start scenes, swapping only
+  the word "fork" ↔ "spoon" changed the first predicted motion far less than it changes
+  the teacher's (0.02–0.13 rad vs 0.39–0.63 rad), and the predicted hand leaned toward the
+  same tray slot in 17 of 20 cases.
+- **Grasping and lifting do sometimes work,** so the earlier statement that "the utensil
+  rose at most 1.1 cm in failed seeds" was wrong: in failed seeds 2, 5, 6 and 8 the
+  requested utensil rose about 8 cm. Seed 6 finished the hand-off and both placements but
+  moved the spare utensil.
+- **The supervisor did not catch every failure.** Most of its "missed grasp" events come
+  from a 15-second no-progress timer, it does not recognise a wrong-object grasp, and its
+  recovery can clear a finished hand-off and opens both hands.
+- **With the supervisor on, no item left the table in the fault run** (5 cups did with it
+  off). With 10 scenes the cause of that difference is not established.
+- **The training cause is not established.** Longer training is one hypothesis; unbalanced
+  demonstrations are another (the teacher succeeded less often when the requested
+  utensil was in tray slot 0 at x = 0.25 m, so the dataset has 346 slot-1 and 223 slot-0
+  demonstrations). Neither has been tested yet.
+
+The raw analysis, scripts and definitions are in
+[`docs/research/2026-09-17-claude-audit/`](docs/research/2026-09-17-claude-audit.md).
+The result folders in `results/` each have a `manifest.json` recording the code
+revision, arguments and package versions.
 
